65	== Specification/basic/operators/opr-overview.tex:254-273
66	
67	\subsection{GCD, LCM, and CHOOSE Operators}
68	
69	The infix operator \OPR{GCD} computes the greatest common
70	divisor of its integer operands, and \OPR{LCM} computes the least
71	common multiple.  The operator \OPR{CHOOSE} computes binomial
72	coefficients:
73	\EXP{n \OPR{CHOOSE} k = \binom{n}{k} = { n! \over k!(n-k)! }}.
74	
75	The results of \OPR{GCD} and \OPR{LCM} are nonnegative
76	(\secref{basic-integers}).
77	On a fixed-size integer type, by the rule for integer results above, a result that the type
78	cannot hold throws an \TYP{IntegerOverflow}: on \EXP{\mathbb{Z}32}, for example,
79	the greatest common divisor of \EXP{-2^{31}} and \EXP{0} is \EXP{2^{31}}, and
80	the least common multiple of \EXP{2^{31}-1} and \EXP{2} is \EXP{2^{32}-2},
81	neither of which \EXP{\mathbb{Z}32} holds.
82	\revision{revival-gcd}{The Working Draft of February 2011 said neither that these
83	results are nonnegative nor what happens when one does not fit its type.}
84	
85	The expression \EXP{e \neq e'} is semantically equivalent to the expression
86	\EXP{\neg(e = e')}.
