88	== Specification/basic/expressions/ranges.tex:12-147
89	
90	\section{Ranges}
91	\seclabel{ranges}
92	
93	\note{Static expressions, static range types, and some range operations are not yet supported.}
94	
95	\begin{Grammar}
96	\emph{Range} &::=& \option{\emph{Expr}}\EXP{:}\option{\emph{Expr}}\options{\EXP{:}\option{\emph{Expr}}}\\
97	&$|$& \emph{Expr}\EXP{\#}\emph{Expr}
98	\end{Grammar}
99	
100	%% :
101	%% ::
102	%% ::c
103	%% :e
104	%% :e:
105	%% :e:e
106	%%
107	%% e:
108	%% e::
109	%% e::e
110	%% e:e
111	%% e:e:
112	%% e:e:e
113	
114	
115	A \emph{range expression} is used to create a special kind of \TYP{Generator} for
116	a set of integers, called a \TYP{Range}, useful
117	for indexing an array or controlling a \KWD{for} loop.  Generators in
118	general are discussed further in \secref{generators}.
119	
120	An \emph{explicit range}
121	is self-contained and completely describes a set of integers.  Its
122	components are values of type \EXP{\mathbb{Z}32}: assume that
123	$a$, $b$, and $c$ are expressions that produce such values.
124	An integer numeral converts to \EXP{\mathbb{Z}32} (\secref{literals}), and a
125	\KWD{nat} or \KWD{int} parameter (\secref{natparams}) used as a component
126	converts to \EXP{\mathbb{Z}32} as an integer numeral does; it is a static error
127	if any other component of an explicit range has an integer type other than
128	\EXP{\mathbb{Z}32}.
129	\revision{revival-inference}{The revival's first revision of this section
130	(\secref{revival-ranges}) left open whether a \KWD{nat} parameter, which may
131	appear wherever a variable of type \EXP{\mathbb{N}32} can (\secref{natparams}),
132	may be a component of a range.
133	The sentence above settles it, as both implementations of the revival already
134	run it: the compiled type checker gives a \KWD{nat} or \KWD{int} parameter used
135	as a value the type of a numeral, and the interpreter makes it a
136	\EXP{\mathbb{Z}32}.}
137	
138	\begin{itemize}
139	\item The range \EXP{a\COLONOP{}b} is the set of $n=\max(0,b-a+1)$ integers $\{a, a+1, a+2, \ldots, b-2, b-1, b\}$.
140	This is a \emph{nonstrided} range.
141	If \VAR{a} and \VAR{b} are both static expressions
142	(described in \secref{static-expr}),
143	then it is a \emph{static range} of type \EXP{\TYP{StaticRange}\llbracket{}a,n,1\rrbracket}
144	and therefore also a \emph{range of static size} of type \EXP{\TYP{RangeOfStaticSize}\llbracket{}n\rrbracket}.
145	
146	\item The range \EXP{a\COLONOP{}b\COLONOP{}c} is
147	the set of $n=\max\left(0, \left\lfloor {b-a+c \over c} \right\rfloor \right)$ integers
148	$\{a, a+c, a+2c, \ldots, a + \left\lfloor {b-a \over c} \right\rfloor c\}$,
149	unless $c$ is zero, in which case it throws an exception.
150	(If $c$ is a static expression, then it is a static error if $c$ is zero.)
151	This is a \emph{strided} range.
152	If \VAR{a}, \VAR{b}, and \VAR{c} are all static expressions,
153	then it is a \emph{static range} of type \EXP{\TYP{StaticRange}\llbracket{}a,n,c\rrbracket}
154	and therefore also a \emph{range of static size} of type \EXP{\TYP{RangeOfStaticSize}\llbracket{}n\rrbracket}.
155	
156	\item The range \EXP{a\mathinner{\hbox{\tt\char'43}}n} is the set of
157	\EXP{\max(0,n)} integers $\{a, a+1, a+2, \ldots, a+n-3, a+n-2, a+n-1\}$.
158	This is a \emph{nonstrided} range.
159	If \VAR{a} and \VAR{n} are both static expressions,
160	then it is a \emph{static range} of type \EXP{\TYP{StaticRange}\llbracket{}a,n,1\rrbracket}
161	If \VAR{n} is a static expression, then it is a \emph{range of static size}
162	of type \EXP{\TYP{RangeOfStaticSize}\llbracket{}n\rrbracket},
163	even if \VAR{a} is not a static expression.
164	\end{itemize}
165	
166	A loop whose counter must have a wider integer type runs over a range of
167	\EXP{\mathbb{Z}32} values and widens each element, as in the following
168	loop, where the binding converts \VAR{i} to \EXP{\mathbb{Z}64}
169	(\secref{basic-integers}):
170	%for i <- 0#n do
171	%  j: ZZ64 = i
172	%  ...
173	%end
174	\begin{Fortress}
175	\(\KWD{for}\:i \leftarrow 0\mathinner{\hbox{\tt\char'43}}n \KWD{do}\)\\
176	{\tt~~}\pushtabs\=\+\(  j\COLON \mathbb{Z}64 = i\)\\
177	\(  \ldots\)\-\\\poptabs
178	\(\KWD{end}\)
179	\end{Fortress}
180	\revision{revival-ranges}{The Working Draft of February 2011 assumed only
181	that the components of an explicit range produce integer values, and named no
182	integer type; two of its own examples built ranges over \EXP{\mathbb{Z}64},
183	and a property of \EXP{\mathbb{Z}} a range over \EXP{\mathbb{Z}}.
184	\Library\ declare the ranges over \EXP{\mathbb{Z}32} alone, as the smaller
185	library of the compiled path already did: a range over another integer type
186	is a static error, and a loop that needs a wider counter widens each element
187	of a range of \EXP{\mathbb{Z}32} values.}
188	
189	Non-static components of a range expression are computed in separate
190	implicit threads.  The range is constructed when all components have
191	completed normally.
192	
193	An \emph{implicit range} may be used only in certain contexts, such as array subscripts,
194	that can supply implicit information.  Suppose an implicit range is used as a subscript
195	for an axis of an array for which the lower bound is $l$ and the upper bound is $u$.
196	\begin{itemize}
197	\item The implicit range \EXP{\COLONOP} is treated as \EXP{l\COLONOP{}u}.
198	\item The implicit range \EXP{\COLONOP\mathrel{\mathtt{:}}c} is
199	  treated as \EXP{l\COLONOP{}u\COLONOP{}c}.
200	\item The implicit range \EXP{\COLONOP{}b} is treated as \EXP{l\COLONOP{}b}.
201	\item The implicit range \EXP{\COLONOP{}b\COLONOP{}c} is treated as
202	\EXP{l\COLONOP{}b\COLONOP{}c}.
203	\item The implicit range \EXP{a\COLONOP} and \EXP{a\mathinner{\hbox{\tt\char'43}}} are treated as \EXP{a\COLONOP{}u}.
204	\item The implicit range \EXP{a\COLONOP\mathrel{\mathtt{:}}c} is treated as
205	\EXP{a\COLONOP{}u\COLONOP{}c}.
206	\item The implicit range \EXP{\mathinner{\hbox{\tt\char'43}}s} is treated as \EXP{l\mathinner{\hbox{\tt\char'43}}s}.
207	\end{itemize}
208	
209	One may test whether an integer is in a range by using the operator $\in$:
210	\input{\home/basic/examples/Expr.Range.tex}
211	
212	Ranges may be compared as if they were sets of integers by using
213	\EXP{\subset} (\STR{SUBSET}) and \EXP{\subseteq} (\STR{SUBSETEQ})
214	and \EXP{=} and \EXP{\supseteq} (\STR{SUPSETEQ}) and \EXP{\supset} (\STR{SUPSET}).
215	
216	Ranges may be intersected using the operator \EXP{\cap} (\STR{INTERSECTION}).
217	
218	The size of a range (the number of integers in the set)
219	may be found by using the set-cardinality operator
220	\EXP{\left|\ldots\right|}.  For example, the value of \EXP{\left|3\COLONOP{}7\right|} is \EXP{5}
221	and the value of \EXP{\left|1\COLONOP{}100\COLONOP{}2\right|} is \EXP{50}.
222	
223	Note that a range is very different from an interval with integer endpoints.
224	The range \EXP{3\COLONOP{}5} contains only the values 3, 4, and 5,
225	whereas the interval \EXP{[3,5]} contains all real numbers \VAR{x} such that \EXP{3\leq{}x\leq{}5}.
